Find the number of rectangles that can be formed inside a fixed regular dodecagon ($12$-gon) where each side of the rectangle lies on either a side or a diagonal of the dodecagon. The total number of such rectangles is even. The diagram below shows three of those rectangles.

<asy>
unitsize(0.6 inch);
for(int i=0; i<360; i+=30) {
dot(dir(i), 4+black);
draw(dir(i)--dir(i+30));
}
draw(dir(120)--dir(330));
filldraw(dir(210)--dir(240)--dir(30)--dir(60)--cycle, mediumgray, linewidth(1.5));
draw((0,0.366)--(0.366,0), linewidth(1.5));
</asy>

Find the largest prime number \(p<1000\) for which there exists a complex number \(z\) satisfying
\begin{itemize}
 \item the real and imaginary part of \(z\) are both integers;
 \item \(|z|=\sqrt{p}\), and
 \item there exists a triangle whose three side lengths are \(p\), the real part of \(z^{3}\), and the imaginary part of \(z^{3}\).
\end{itemize}
In addition, \(p\equiv 3\pmod{4}\).

